n you cut your chicken it's  uniform all the way through because if  you start with a piece that's like too  wide at one end and too narrow the other  you might get like halfway into the  bread and then it's going to be like  nope i don't want to go in anymore and  then we'll stop once you perfectly fit  the chicken and the cheese inside the  baguette you want to make sure that you  line it all up cut them into about an  inch pieces because this is meant to be  hand-held food once you cut them up line  it on the tray brush some amazing garlic  butter that you make over the top which  is super super easy just some garlic  just some butter maybe some parsley if  you want for color be liberal you know  garlic bread whoever's eating it i don't  think they're trying to be on a diet so  and you don't and you also don't have to  tell them how much you put in it so  brush liberally it'll get absorbed it'll  be delicious nobody's gonna complain  everybody loves garlic breads after you  brush it with butter cover it with foil  or don't depending on how crispy you  want your garlic bread to bake it off  and you have this wonderful wonderful  finger food for your party what's great  about it is because you've made these  cuts and because you put them back  together in the oven the cheese is  actually going to help it all stick  together so when it comes to the table  and people pull it apart you get that  amazing signature tasty cheese pull that  i've spent way too long trying to get  sometimes i think you serve it with a  side of marinara sauce you have all the  elements of chicken parmesan and garlic  bread in one single bite which i think  is really really cool because it's a  handheld version of a usual dish that  requires a fork and a knife so i think  this is a great idea i wasn't sure if it  was gonna be too much work where  everybody was gonna be like this is  stupid like why would you ever do this  but eventually one day i was like you  know what alvin if you're not go